{
  "id": "r10",
  "text": "There are two stories here, and the more glamorous one can swallow the other. Silent Hills is the game people imagine: the names, the possibilities, the perfect horror project that never had to survive being released. P.T. is the thing we can actually examine. We can watch how it guides a camera, compare the changing corridor, argue about the puzzles, and look inside the software for decisions an ordinary player never sees. It doesn't need to be flawless to be worth keeping. That is what makes its disappearance from the normal download path more interesting than a cancelled marketing campaign. The advertisement became an experience with a life of its own. First, people shared it to discover what it was. Later, they worried about whether they would still be able to reach it. The hallway didn't vanish from every console. What vanished was the reassuring assumption that you could always come back. And for a game built around walking through the same door again, that is a remarkably fitting thing to lose.",
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
